  "created_at": "2026-08-26T10:34:46Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-08-26T10:34:46Z",
  "body": "Closing because this no longer applies to `main`, not because the reasoning was\nwrong. The premise held: `-fcf-protection=full` was unconditional and it *did*\nbreak both aarch64 builds, which is exactly where #8 started.\n\n**What landed in #8.** The flag is selected from `$(CC) -dumpmachine` rather\nthan being unconditional, so it is never passed to an aarch64 compiler. It is\n`-dumpmachine` and not `uname -m` because the aarch64 artefacts are\ncross-compiled: the machine running `make` is x86-64 while the object being\nproduced is not. Measured both ways, x86-64 `gl-fwd.so` carries 3478 `endbr64`\nand aarch64 carries 0. That is why this diff now conflicts: the three literal\noccurrences it removes are one `$(CET_CFLAGS)`.\n\n**And a build without it exists.** `sh scripts/build.sh --portable` drops the\nflag outright, and ships in the `v0.1.0` assets alongside the default. It was\nadded for a different request, but it is the build for a consumer who would\nrather not see the instructions at all.\n\n**The measurement behind that**, in `docs/REPORT.md` 9.13: on bullseye,\nbookworm and trixie the flag emits `endbr64` and **no** IBT property note. The\nnote cannot be emitted honestly, because `GNU_PROPERTY_X86_FEATURE_1_AND` is\nANDed across every input and glibc's `crti.o` carries no property on any of the\nthree. Without the note a CET-enforcing loader does not turn IBT on for the\nobject, so on those images the instructions do no protective work. \u26a0 Removing\nthe flag also does not remove every `endbr64`: the trampolines supply theirs\nwhatever the flag does, and it removes six.\n\n**What this does not settle** is your actual question, which is whether the\n*default* build should carry it at all given the kernels and emulation layers\nthis ends up under, including the FreeBSD linuxulator. That is a policy call\nfor the maintainers rather than something #8 decided, and the `--portable`\nvariant sidesteps it rather than answering it. Reopen or raise it fresh against\ncurrent `main` if you want that decision made.",
