  "created_at": "2026-08-26T10:36:08Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-08-26T10:36:08Z",
  "body": "Done in #8, shipped in [`v0.1.0`](https://github.com/pkgforge-dev/cross-libc-dlopen/releases/tag/v0.1.0).\n\u26a0 Three of the four asks landed as written. **The fourth did not, and that is a\ndecision rather than an oversight**, so it is spelled out below rather than\nleft for you to find.\n\n| asked for | on `main` |\n|---|---|\n| remove `CLD_MARKER_NAMES` and the marker loop in `cross_libc_dlopen_mode()` | \u2705 gone |\n| enabled by default when preloaded, `CROSS_LIBC_DLOPEN=0` disables | \u2705 `mode()` is four lines: read the variable, return 0 if it is `0`, otherwise 1 |\n| remove the `ANYLINUX_*` aliases from `cld_getenv()` | \u2705 gone from `src/` entirely |\n| remove `CLD_ENV_ROOT_ALT` / the `APPDIR` fallback | \u26a0 **kept by default, removable at build time** |\n\n**Why `APPDIR` stayed.** Your argument for the markers applies to it and I\nthink it is right: preloading is a deliberate act and a silent no-op is a\nfootgun. The difference is who pays. An AppImage runtime exports `APPDIR` into\nevery process it starts, and **a library cannot know whether it is inside one**;\nwhoever assembles the bundle can. Dropping it by default would move the\nsilent-failure class rather than remove it, from \"forgot the marker\" to\n\"forgot that the runtime already told us where the root is\".\n\nSo it is a build-time choice, and the one spelling you asked for is one flag:\n\n```\nmake CFLAGS='-O2 -DCLD_STRICT_ENV'      # reads CROSS_LIBC_DLOPEN_ROOT and nothing else\nsh scripts/build.sh --portable          # the same, and it is in the v0.1.0 assets\n```\n\n`cross-libc-dlopen-portable-x86_64` and `-aarch64` are that build. \u2b50 **Both\narms are measured, not asserted:** E87 requires `appdir : (unset)` under\n`-DCLD_STRICT_ENV` and E88 requires it populated without, so neither can\nsilently stop working.\n\n\u26d4 **One thing worth flagging, because it changes what this project is to you.**\nThe `demo` AppImage now ships `lib/cross-libc-dlopen.so` where it used to ship\n`lib/foreign-dlopen.so`, and its `.preload` names `gl-fwd.so`, `egl-fwd.so` and\n`gles-fwd.so` too. That is this project adopted upstream, which is the outcome\nthis issue was arguing towards. It also broke the suite's A/B: E30 and E37a\nexist to show the *as-shipped* arm finding no device, and it now finds one,\nbecause the shipped dispatcher is this project. Recorded in `docs/REPORT.md`\n9.17 and left open deliberately, since flipping those predictions would turn\ntwo controls into two cases that pass whatever the shim does.\n\nReopen if the `APPDIR` reasoning does not hold for a bundle you actually build.\nThat is the part I am least able to check from here.",
